PRACTICAL 1 – SET OPERATIONS

Question

Write a program in Python to implement Set Operations using Python to perform union, intersection, difference, and Cartesian product on datasets.

Program

A = {1, 2, 3, 4}
B = {3, 4, 5, 6}

print("Union:", A | B)
print("Intersection:", A & B)
print("Difference:", A - B)
print("Cartesian Product:", [(x, y) for x in A for y in B])

Algorithm

Start the program.

Create two sets A and B.

Perform union of both sets.

Perform intersection of both sets.

Perform difference of A and B.

Calculate the Cartesian product.

Display all results.

Stop the program.

Explanation

A | B – Combines all unique elements from both sets.

A & B – Returns common elements.

A - B – Returns elements present in A but not in B.

[(x, y) for x in A for y in B] – Generates all possible ordered pairs.

Mathematical Solution

Given:

A = {1, 2, 3, 4}

B = {3, 4, 5, 6}

1. Union

A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}

2. Intersection

A ∩ B = {3, 4}

3. Difference

A − B = {1, 2}

4. Cartesian Product

A × B = {
(1,3), (1,4), (1,5), (1,6),
(2,3), (2,4), (2,5), (2,6),
(3,3), (3,4), (3,5), (3,6),
(4,3), (4,4), (4,5), (4,6)
}

PRACTICAL 2 – RELATION PROPERTIES

Question

Write a program in Python to develop algorithms to verify if a given set of ordered pairs represents a reflexive, symmetric, transitive, or equivalence relation.

Program

A = {1, 2}
R = {(1, 1), (2, 2), (1, 2), (2, 1)}

reflexive = all((x, x) in R for x in A)
symmetric = all((y, x) in R for x, y in R)

transitive = all(
    (x, z) in R
    for x, y in R
    for a, z in R
    if y == a
)

print("Reflexive:", reflexive)
print("Symmetric:", symmetric)
print("Transitive:", transitive)
print("Equivalence Relation:", reflexive and symmetric and transitive)

Algorithm

Start the program.

Define a set A and relation R.

Check whether every element has a self-pair.

Check whether every pair has its reverse pair.

Check whether the relation satisfies transitivity.

Check whether all three properties are true.

Display the results.

Stop the program.

Explanation

all() – Checks whether all conditions are true.

(x, x) – Checks reflexive property.

(y, x) – Checks symmetric property.

Transitivity checks whether (x,y) and (y,z) imply (x,z).

Equivalence relation requires all three properties.

Mathematical Solution

Given:

A = {1, 2}

R = {(1,1), (2,2), (1,2), (2,1)}

1. Reflexive

A relation is reflexive if every element is related to itself.

(1,1) ∈ R

(2,2) ∈ R

Therefore, Reflexive = True.

2. Symmetric

A relation is symmetric if (a,b) ∈ R implies (b,a) ∈ R.

(1,2) and (2,1) both exist.

Therefore, Symmetric = True.

3. Transitive

A relation is transitive if:

(a,b) ∈ R and (b,c) ∈ R imply (a,c) ∈ R.

All required combinations are present in R.

Therefore, Transitive = True.

4. Equivalence Relation

An equivalence relation must be reflexive, symmetric and transitive.

Therefore, R is an Equivalence Relation.

PRACTICAL 3 – INJECTIVE, SURJECTIVE AND BIJECTIVE FUNCTIONS

Question

Write a program in Python to implement injective, surjective, and bijective functions to map inputs to outputs in data transformation scenarios.

Program

A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {2, 4, 6}

f = {1: 2, 2: 4, 3: 6}

values = list(f.values())

injective = len(values) == len(set(values))
surjective = set(values) == B
bijective = injective and surjective

print("Injective:", injective)
print("Surjective:", surjective)
print("Bijective:", bijective)

Algorithm

Start the program.

Define the input set A.

Define the output set B.

Create a mapping between A and B.

Check whether all outputs are unique.

Check whether every element of B is mapped.

Check whether both conditions are true.

Display the results.

Stop the program.

Explanation

f – Stores the mapping of inputs to outputs.

f.values() – Returns all output values.

set(values) – Removes duplicate values.

len(values) == len(set(values)) – Checks injectivity.

set(values) == B – Checks surjectivity.

injective and surjective – Checks bijectivity.

Mathematical Solution

Given:

A = {1, 2, 3}

B = {2, 4, 6}

Function:

f(1) = 2

f(2) = 4

f(3) = 6

1. Injective (One-to-One)

Every input has a different output.

All outputs are unique: 2, 4, 6.

Therefore, Injective = True.

2. Surjective (Onto)

Every element of B must have at least one input mapping to it.

All elements 2, 4, 6 are mapped.

Therefore, Surjective = True.

3. Bijective

A function is bijective if it is both injective and surjective.

Therefore, Bijective = True.

