PRACTICAL 5 – VARIANCE AND STANDARD DEVIATION

Question

Write a program in Python to compute the variance and standard deviation to understand data spread and identify outliers.

Program

import statistics as st

data = [10, 20, 30, 40, 100]

# Variance
v = st.variance(data)

# Standard Deviation
sd = st.stdev(data)

# Outliers
outliers = [x for x in data if abs(x - st.mean(data)) > 2 * sd]

print("Variance:", v)
print("Standard Deviation:", round(sd, 2))
print("Outliers:", outliers)

Algorithm

Start the program.

Import the statistics library.

Create a dataset.

Calculate the variance.

Calculate the standard deviation.

Calculate the mean of the dataset.

Identify values that differ from the mean by more than two standard deviations.

Display variance, standard deviation and outliers.

Stop the program.

Explanation

import statistics as st – Imports the statistics library with the short name st.

st.variance(data) – Calculates sample variance.

st.stdev(data) – Calculates sample standard deviation.

st.mean(data) – Calculates the mean.

abs() – Calculates the absolute difference.

round(sd, 2) – Rounds the standard deviation to two decimal places.

The list comprehension identifies values more than two standard deviations away from the mean.

Mathematical Solution

Given dataset:

D = {10, 20, 30, 40, 100}

1. Mean

Mean = (10 + 20 + 30 + 40 + 100) / 5

Mean = 200 / 5 = 40

2. Variance (Sample)

Formula:

s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / (n − 1)

Substituting values:

s² = [(10−40)² + (20−40)² + (30−40)² + (40−40)² + (100−40)²] / 4

s² = (900 + 400 + 100 + 0 + 3600) / 4

s² = 5000 / 4

Variance = 1250

3. Standard Deviation

s = √1250

Standard Deviation ≈ 35.36

4. Outlier Detection

The program uses the two-standard-deviation rule:

|x − Mean| > 2 × Standard Deviation

For 100:

|100 − 40| = 60

2 × 35.36 = 70.72

Since 60 is less than 70.72, 100 is not classified as an outlier by this particular rule.

Therefore, Outliers = [].

